          "statement": "Let $G=\\mathbb Z_2=\\{0,1\\}$ with addition modulo $2$. Let $\\operatorname{Vec}_G$ be the category of finite-dimensional complex $G$-graded vector spaces $W=W_0\\oplus W_1$, with grade-preserving linear maps and tensor product\n$$(W\\otimes U)_c=\\bigoplus_{a+b=c}W_a\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}U_b.$$\nIts tensor unit is $\\delta_0=\\mathbb C$ in grade $0$; write $\\delta_1=\\mathbb C$ in grade $1$. The simple objects are $\\delta_0,\\delta_1$, with $\\delta_a\\otimes\\delta_b\\cong\\delta_{a+b}$. The object $D=\\delta_0\\oplus\\delta_1$ satisfies\n$$D\\otimes D\\cong 2\\delta_0\\oplus2\\delta_1.$$\nFor any $W$, its left and right duals are the graded vector space $W^*$ with $(W^*)_a=(W_{-a})^*$. The left evaluation and coevaluation have types $\\operatorname{ev}_L:W^*\\otimes W\\to\\delta_0$ and $\\operatorname{coev}_L:\\delta_0\\to W\\otimes W^*$, with $\\operatorname{ev}_L(\\phi\\otimes w)=\\phi(w)$ and $\\operatorname{coev}_L(1)=\\sum_{a,i}e_{a,i}\\otimes e^{a,i}$. The right evaluation and coevaluation have types $\\operatorname{ev}_R:W\\otimes W^*\\to\\delta_0$ and $\\operatorname{coev}_R:\\delta_0\\to W^*\\otimes W$, with $\\operatorname{ev}_R(w\\otimes\\phi)=\\phi(w)$ and $\\operatorname{coev}_R(1)=\\sum_{a,i}e^{a,i}\\otimes e_{a,i}$, where $e_{a,i}$ is a basis of $W_a$ and $e^{a,i}$ its dual basis, placed in grade $-a$ of $W^*$. Evaluations vanish on homogeneous pairs whose degrees do not sum to $0$. The canonical associator is vector-space rebracketing. These formulas give both dual orientations; $\\delta_0$ has itself as both duals with identity maps.\n\nThus $D$ is non-invertible although each of its two simple summands is tensor-invertible. The object $D$ is reducible; this example does not assert the existence of a non-invertible simple object or a physical defect realization.",
