          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "statement": "Let $\\Gamma$ be the boundary graph of a square with vertices $v_0,v_1,v_2,v_3$ and one plaquette, traversed in the order $v_0\\to v_1\\to v_2\\to v_3\\to v_0$. Label its successive boundary edges $e_1,e_2,e_3,e_4$; choose their reference orientations to be $v_0\\to v_1$, $v_1\\to v_2$, $v_3\\to v_2$, and $v_0\\to v_3$, respectively. Use the compact matrix group $G=SU(2)=\\left\\{\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\-\\overline b&\\overline a\\end{pmatrix}:a,b\\in\\mathbb C,\\ |a|^2+|b|^2=1\\right\\}$ in its defining two-dimensional representation. Assign a link variable $U_j\\in G$ to each edge in its reference orientation; in the ordered boundary product, a reversed traversal contributes $U_j^{-1}$. Thus the plaquette holonomy is $U_p=U_1U_2U_3^{-1}U_4^{-1}$. The group is the unit sphere $S^3$ in the $(a,b)$ coordinates; let $\\mu$ be its normalized round surface measure. For $\\beta\\ge0$, define the dimensionless action $S_\\beta(U)=\\beta\\left(1-\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Re}\\operatorname{Tr}U_p\\right)$ and partition function $Z_\\beta=\\int_{G^4}e^{-S_\\beta(U)}\\,d\\mu^{\\otimes4}(U)$. This finite graph integral satisfies $e^{-2\\beta}\\le Z_\\beta\\le1$, so it is finite and strictly positive. It has only four group variables and defines no continuum fields, no Wightman or Osterwalder–Schrader reconstruction, and no Hamiltonian; it therefore does not solve the four-dimensional existence and mass-gap problem.",
