r factor acts by the standard and dual representations. Here $SO^+(1,3)=\\{L\\in GL_4(\\mathbb R):L^T\\eta L=\\eta,\\det L=1,L^0{}_0\\ge1\\}$, and the physical Lorentz and gauge groups map to $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$ by $(L,u)\\mapsto(L,u)$, viewing $L$ as a complex matrix and $u\\in SU(2)$ as a matrix in $SL(2,\\mathbb C)$; the standard and dual actions restrict to the specified physical actions. Impose $\\widetilde H=H^\\dagger$ for the real action. Take no flavor symmetry, field weight one, derivative cutoff $r=0$, maximum field degree $q=6$, retained engineering dimension $D_{\\max}=6$, loop order $L_{\\max}=0$, and a positive scalar coefficient scale $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}$ of mass dimension one as assigned by the physical EFT operator contract. For this illustration the declared relation subspace is zero. The quotient uses $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$ as a formal algebraic selection only; no equality with the full invariant subspace for the specified physical symmetry group is asserted. Then $\\mathcal O_2=\\widetilde H H$, $\\mathcal O_4=(\\widetilde H H)^2$, and $\\mathcal O_6=(\\widetilde H H)^3$ are gauge-invariant scalar polynomials of engineering dimensions $2$, $4$, and $6$. With real dimensionless $C_6$, the term $C_6\\mathcal O_6/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^2$ has Lagrangian density dimension $4$ and belongs to this dimension-six truncation. For $a,b\\in\\{1,2\\}$, define $\\varepsilon_{12}=1$, $\\varepsilon_{21}=-1$, and $\\varepsilon_{11}=\\varepsilon_{22}=0$; then the antisymmetric polynomial $\\varepsilon_{ab}H^aH^b$ vanishes because the even field components commute. This is a finite scalar-sector illustration, not a complete $SU(2)$ EFT basis or a claim that these coefficients describe nature.\n\n",
