          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "M. F. Atiyah and I. M. Singer, The Index of Elliptic Operators: I, Annals of Mathematics 87 (1968), 484–530",
                "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~shmuel/tom-readings/ASI.pdf",
                "locator": "§8, printed pp. 525–526, the Hardy projection and rectangle clutching computation."
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume AC. Let $S^1=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$, with coordinate $x$ and density $dx/(2\\pi)$. Let a compact Lie group $G$ act trivially on $S^1$ and let $V$ be a finite-dimensional unitary complex $G$-representation, acting pointwise on $L^2(S^1;V)$. Write $R(G)$ for the Grothendieck group of finite-dimensional continuous complex $G$-representations. For a bounded $G$-equivariant operator $Q:\\mathcal H_0\\to\\mathcal H_1$ between Hilbert $G$-representations, Fredholm means that $\\ker Q$ is finite dimensional, $\\operatorname{ran}Q$ is closed, and $\\operatorname{coker}Q:=\\mathcal H_1/\\operatorname{ran}Q$ is finite dimensional. The quotient representation is unitarily isomorphic to $\\ker Q^*$, where $Q^*$ is the Hilbert adjoint, and we set $\\operatorname{Ind}_G(Q)=[\\ker Q]-[\\operatorname{coker}Q]=[\\ker Q]-[\\ker Q^*]\\in R(G)$. In the finite-chart classical calculus, an order-zero operator has localized symbols satisfying $|\\partial_x^\\alpha\\partial_\\xi^\\beta p(x,\\xi)|\\le C_{\\alpha\\beta K}\\langle\\xi\\rangle^{-|\\beta|}$ on compact coordinate subsets, with a smooth leading symbol $p_0$ on nonzero cotangent vectors satisfying $p_0(x,t\\xi)=p_0(x,\\xi)$ for $t>0$; ellipticity means that $p_0(x,\\xi)$ is invertible at every such vector. On $L^2(S^1;V)$ let $P_+$ be the orthogonal projection onto the closed span of $e^{inx}v$ with $n\\ge0$. The bounded operator\n\n$$A=e^{ix}P_++(1-P_+)$$\n\nis an equivariant order-zero elliptic pseudodifferential operator. Its leading symbol on the two components of the cotangent sphere is $e^{ix}I_V$ at $\\xi>0$ and $I_V$ at $\\xi<0$. Its kernel is zero, its cokernel is the constant copy of $V$, and $\\operatorname{Ind}_G(A)=-[V]$.\n\nFor a locally compact space $Z$, define $K_c^0(Z)$ to be the Grothendieck group under block sum of pairs of finite-rank complex vector bundles over $Z$ and continuous bundle maps that are isomorphisms outside a compact subset, modulo bundle isomorphism, homotopies with a common compact subset outside which every map is invertible, and addition or removal of everywhere-invertible pairs. Write $b\\in K_c^0(T^*\\mathbb R)$ for the class of the pair of trivial complex lines over $T^*\\mathbb R\\cong\\mathbb R^2$ whose map is multiplication by $z=x+i\\xi$, invertible outside the origin. Its one-point compactification $(T^*\\mathbb R)^+\\cong S^2$ identifies this relative group with $\\widetilde K^0(S^2)$, the virtual complex-bundle classes of rank zero at the compactification point. On $|z|=1$, the normalized map $z/|z|=e^{i\\vartheta}$ is the clutching transition. Identify $S^2=\\mathbb{CP}^1$ with the set of complex lines $\\ell\\subset\\mathbb C^2$, and let $\\gamma=\\{(\\ell,v):v\\in\\ell\\}$ be its tautological line bundle. Choose hemispheric trivializations whose transition is $g(e^{i\\vartheta})=e^{i\\vartheta}$. With this convention, $b$ is $\\beta=[\\gamma]-[\\underline{\\mathbb C}]=[\\gamma]-1$, where $\\underline{\\mathbb C}$ is the trivial line over $S^2$. A realization on a compact ambient base is controlled if its Schwartz kernel agrees with the exterior bundle isomorphism's multiplication kernel whenever either kernel variable lies outside a compact set. Define the controlled analytic index of a compact-support class to be the Fredholm index $[\\ker P]-[\\ker P^*]$ of a G-invariant elliptic realization on a compact ambient base. After a homotopy through degree-zero homogeneous principal symbols, the symbol of $A$ differs from the identity only over a compact coordinate interval, and its compact-support class there is $-b\\otimes V$. Thus the compact-support analytic index of $b$ is $1$.\n\nFor clarity about orientation, the tautological cotangent form is $\\theta=\\xi\\,dx$ and $d\\theta=d\\xi\\wedge dx$. The positively oriented boundary for the complex plane $x+i\\xi$ traverses the top of a rectangle from right to left. The Hardy symbol winds $-1$ on that boundary. Reversing to the $d\\theta$ orientation reverses both boundary windings; it does not change the class identity $[\\sigma(A)]=-b\\otimes V$.",
