          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal B$ be the graded-commutative canonical field/antifield algebra of the preceding definition: $|\\Phi^A|=\\epsilon_A$, $\\operatorname{gh}(\\Phi^A)=g_A$, $|\\Phi_A^*|=\\epsilon_A+1$, and $\\operatorname{gh}(\\Phi_A^*)=-1-g_A$. Its canonical antibracket is odd, has ghost number $+1$, and satisfies the shifted skew and Jacobi identities stated there. Assume the center of $\\mathcal B$ contains no nonzero element of ghost number one. In the finite-dimensional coordinate algebra this follows because a central element has zero derivative with respect to every field and antifield coordinate and is therefore a numerical constant; for a formal local-functional domain it is an explicit hypothesis. For any even $S_{\\mathrm{BV}}\\in\\mathcal B$ of ghost number zero, define $sF:=(S_{\\mathrm{BV}},F)$. Then $s^2=0$ on $\\mathcal B$ if and only if the classical master equation $(S_{\\mathrm{BV}},S_{\\mathrm{BV}})=0$ holds.\n\nIn the local-field case of the preceding definition, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty and open, let $\\mathfrak g$ be a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra, and let $\\rho$ be the stated parity-preserving representation on the graded matter space. Take the minimal fields $A,\\psi,c$ and their antifields, with $\\delta_\\lambda A_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu\\lambda+[A_\\mu,\\lambda]$ and $\\delta_\\lambda\\psi=-\\rho(\\lambda)\\psi$ for every admitted smooth $\\lambda:U\\to\\mathfrak g$. Assume $[\\delta_\\lambda,\\delta_\\mu]=\\delta_{[\\lambda,\\mu]}$ without equations of motion, where $[\\lambda,\\mu](x):=[\\lambda(x),\\mu(x)]_{\\mathfrak g}$, and that $\\delta_\\lambda\\Phi\\equiv0$ for every field configuration implies $\\lambda\\equiv0$. Let $S_0[A,\\psi]$ be a local functional with well-defined variational derivatives, invariant under these transformations. Choose a functional domain and support or boundary conditions for which the antibracket, integrations by parts, and gauge-variation identity are well-defined and boundary terms vanish. For $s_0A_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c]$, $s_0c=-\\tfrac12[c,c]$, and $s_0\\psi=-\\rho(c)\\psi$, form the minimal action $S_{\\mathrm{BV}}=S_0+\\int_U d^nx\\,\\sum_A(-1)^{\\epsilon_A}\\Phi_A^*s_0\\Phi^A$ in the order specified in the definition. Then this minimal action satisfies the classical master equation $(S_{\\mathrm{BV}},S_{\\mathrm{BV}})=0$.",
