          "statement": "Let $\\mathfrak g=\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ with basis $T_a=-\\tfrac{i}{2}\\sigma_a$, where\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen $[T_a,T_b]=\\epsilon_{ab}{}^cT_c$. Use the defining representation on $V=\\mathbb C^2$, so $\\rho(T_a)=T_a$, and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be a nonempty open set, where $n$ is a nonnegative integer. Place $V$ wholly in either even or odd parity. For $n\\geq1$, use the finite-jet algebra of the earlier BRST definition. For $n=0$, $U=\\mathbb R^0$ is a point: take the graded-commutative algebra over $\\mathbb C$ freely generated by the two matter components of the chosen parity and three odd ghosts, with no gauge-potential components or positive-order jets. On these algebras, with even gauge potential $A_\\mu=A_\\mu^aT_a$, odd ghosts $c^a$, and homogeneous matter components $\\psi^\\alpha$, define the odd left derivation $s$ by $sf=0$ on coefficient functions, by prolongation $s(D_I\\Phi)=D_I(s\\Phi)$ on jets, and by\n\n$$sA_\\mu^a=\\partial_\\mu c^a+\\epsilon^a{}_{bc}A_\\mu^bc^c,\\qquad sc^a=-\\frac12\\epsilon^a{}_{bc}c^bc^c,\\qquad s\\psi=-c^aT_a\\psi.$$\n\nThe operator $s$ squares to zero on these generators and their formal spacetime jets.",
