          "statement": "Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ and Minkowski signature $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-,+,+,+)$. In four spacetime dimensions assign mass dimensions $[x^\\mu]=-1$, $[\\partial_\\mu]=1$, $[A_\\mu]=1$, $[F_{\\mu\\nu}]=2$, $[\\psi_f]=3/2$, $[e_{\\mathrm{DS}}]=[\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}]=0$, and $[m_e]=[M_\\mu]=[\\mu]=[Q]=1$. For $x\\in\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, regard $A_\\mu(x)$, $\\psi_e(x)$, and $\\psi_\\mu(x)$ as formal perturbative fields, with no classical-solution regularity or nonperturbative operator domain assumed. The potential $A=A_\\mu dx^\\mu$ is a real $U(1)$ one-form and $F=dA$ has components $F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$. The fields $\\psi_e,\\psi_\\mu$ are four-component complex Dirac spinors; use Clifford matrices satisfying $\\{\\gamma^\\mu,\\gamma^\\nu\\}=-2\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}$ and the Dirac adjoint $\\bar{\\psi}=\\psi^\\dagger\\gamma^0$. At each fixed renormalization scale $\\mu$ in an active interval, let $e_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)>0$ and use $D_\\nu=\\partial_\\nu+ie_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)A_\\nu$ for fields of charge $-e_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)$, with gauge kinetic term $-\\tfrac14F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}$. For a real dimensionless gauge parameter $\\chi(x)$, the convention is $A\\mapsto A+d\\chi$ and $\\psi_f\\mapsto e^{-ie_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)\\chi}\\psi_f$. Define the dimensionless running coupling by $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)=e_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)^2/(4\\pi)$. The decoupling-subtraction (DS) convention uses minimal subtraction within each interval of active charged fields, removes a charged field from the low-energy theory below its threshold, and matches the coupling continuously at that threshold. Set $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(m_e)=\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}>0$ as the low-energy boundary condition. The source-reported one-loop DS flow has two active Dirac fields for $\\mu>M_\\mu$, one for $m_e<\\mu<M_\\mu$, and none for $\\mu<m_e$. For $q>0$, define $R_e(q)=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{q^2}{m_e^2}\\right)$ and $R_\\mu(q)=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{M_\\mu^2}{m_e^2}\\right)-\\frac{2}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{q^2}{M_\\mu^2}\\right)$. For positive evaluation scale $Q$ of mass dimension one, require $R_e(q)>0$ for every $q\\in[m_e,Q]$ when $m_e<Q<M_\\mu$; for $Q>M_\\mu$, require $R_e(q)>0$ for every $q\\in[m_e,M_\\mu]$ and $R_\\mu(q)>0$ for every $q\\in[M_\\mu,Q]$. The corresponding one-loop solutions are:\n$$\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(Q)}=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{Q^2}{m_e^2}\\right)\\qquad(m_e<Q<M_\\mu),$$\nand\n$$\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(Q)}=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{M_\\mu^2}{m_e^2}\\right)-\\frac{2}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{Q^2}{M_\\mu^2}\\right)\\qquad(Q>M_\\mu).$$\nThe two expressions agree continuously at $Q=M_\\mu$. These are solutions of the supplied one-loop renormalization-group equations; they are not measured values, exact all-orders predictions, or numerical error estimates.",
