          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(S,\\mathcal S)$ be a measurable data space, let $\\Theta$ be a nonempty parameter set, and let $(\\mathbb P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}$ be a family of probability measures on $(S,\\mathcal S)$; these data form the statistical model. Let $\\Theta_0\\subseteq\\Theta$ be a nonempty null set and let $T:S\\to\\mathbb R$ be a measurable statistic, meaning $T^{-1}(A)\\in\\mathcal S$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)$; in particular every upper-tail set is measurable. Fix an explicit calibration probability measure $Q_0$ on $(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$ for the statistic under the null procedure. For a simple null $\\Theta_0=\\{\\theta_0\\}$, the exact calibration is $Q_0=\\mathbb P_{\\theta_0}\\circ T^{-1}$, meaning $Q_0(A)=\\mathbb P_{\\theta_0}(T^{-1}(A))$ for every Borel set $A$. For a composite null or an asymptotic analysis, $Q_0$ is supplied by the specified calibration procedure, whose nuisance-parameter treatment and approximation assumptions are part of the data.\n\nFor an observed datum $s\\in S$, with the convention that larger $T$ values are more extreme, the upper-tail p-value is\n$$p_0(s):=Q_0\\bigl(\\{t\\in\\mathbb R:t\\ge T(s)\\}\\bigr).$$\nIt is a probability under the stated null calibration for a test statistic at least as extreme as the observed one. It is not a probability assigned to the null hypothesis conditional on the data; no probability distribution on $\\Theta$ is supplied by this definition. The definition alone establishes neither type-I error control nor super-uniformity; any such validity claim requires a separate argument for the specified sampling model and calibration. A lower-tail or two-sided procedure must specify its own measurable extremeness set instead of using the displayed upper-tail convention.",
