            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Let $M$ be an oriented smooth manifold of finite dimension $n$, with integration cycle $M$, smooth positive volume form $\\mu$, graded algebra $\\mathcal A=\\Omega^\\bullet(M;\\mathbb C)$, and integral $I_\\Gamma(F)=\\int_M F_{[n]}$ of the degree-$n$ part. Let $Q$ be a smooth odd derivation of $\\mathcal A$, let $S,O$ be smooth even forms, let $V$ be a smooth odd form, and let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval. Suppose $QS=QO=0$, $(Q^2V)e^{-S-tQV}=0$ for every $t\\in J$, and $I_\\Gamma(Q(O V e^{-S-tQV}))=0$ for every $t\\in J$, including the zero-boundary-flux condition. Write $(Oe^{-S-tQV})_{[n]}=f_t\\mu$. Suppose that $f_t$ is integrable for each $t\\in J$, is differentiable in $t$ almost everywhere with measurable derivative, and that for every compact $K\\subset J$ one integrable majorant $g_K$ bounds both $|f_t|$ and $|\\partial_t f_t|$ for all $t\\in K$, outside one null set. Then\n\n$$Z(t):=I_\\Gamma(Oe^{-S-tQV})=\\int_M f_t\\,d\\mu$$\n\nis constant on $J$, and for every $t\\in J$,\n\n$$Z'(t)=-I_\\Gamma(O(QV)e^{-S-tQV})=-I_\\Gamma(Q(O V e^{-S-tQV}))=0.$$\n\nIn particular, the conclusion is finite-dimensional and applies only at finite parameter values in $J$; it gives no $t\\to\\infty$ saddle-point limit and no identity for an infinite-dimensional continuum functional integral.",
