          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $B(v)$ and $\\nu_0=2m^2>0$ be the pole-subtracted forward-amplitude data of the amplitude-input definition. In addition to being even and holomorphic near $v=0$, retain its real-analyticity condition $B(\\overline v)=\\overline{B(v)}$. If $B(v)=\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\beta_jv^j$ near $0$, uniqueness of the Taylor expansion gives $\\beta_j=\\overline{\\beta_j}$ from this condition and $\\beta_{2n+1}=0$ from evenness. Hence the even Taylor coefficients are real. Fix an integer truncation order $N\\ge1$. For $n\\ge1$ define the Taylor coefficient $c_n=B^{(2n)}(0)/(2n)!$ and the dimensionless forward on-shell coefficient $b_k=\\nu_0^{2k+2}c_{k+1}\\in\\mathbb R$ for $0\\le k\\le N$. Since $v$ and $\\nu_0$ have units $(\\text{mass})^2$, $c_n$ has units $(\\text{mass})^{-4n}$ and $b_k$ is dimensionless. Here “Wilson coefficient” refers only to this normalized on-shell amplitude coordinate; it does not identify a generic coefficient of an off-shell Lagrangian operator basis.\n\nFor each integer $r\\ge0$ with $2r\\le N$, form the quadratic constraint\n\n$$\\sum_{i,j=0}^{r}a_i a_j b_{i+j}\\ge0\\qquad\\text{for every }(a_0,\\ldots,a_r)\\in\\mathbb R^{r+1}.$$\n\nFor each integer $r\\ge0$ with $2r+1\\le N$, form the two additional quadratic constraints\n\n$$\\sum_{i,j=0}^{r}a_i a_j b_{i+j+1}\\ge0,\\qquad \\sum_{i,j=0}^{r}a_i a_j\\bigl(b_{i+j}-b_{i+j+1}\\bigr)\\ge0\\qquad\\text{for every }(a_0,\\ldots,a_r)\\in\\mathbb R^{r+1}.$$\n\nThe **finite order-$N$ forward-amplitude bootstrap feasible set** $\\mathcal K_N$ is the subset of $\\mathbb R^{N+1}$ consisting of vectors $(b_0,\\ldots,b_N)$ that satisfy every applicable constraint above. The finite truncated bootstrap problem is to determine whether a supplied coefficient vector belongs to $\\mathcal K_N$. Passing this finite test is a necessary condition supplied by forward positivity; it is not defined to be sufficient for the existence of a full amplitude-data package as defined above.",
